00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.917
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.330
A disaster typically means a
service-interrupting event.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.690
So what is disaster
recovery or DR?

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.270
Well, it's the amount
of impact a business

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.760
can take during a disaster.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.870
We use two key metrics
to define the impact.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.090
RTO, or recovery
time objective, which

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.820
is the maximum amount
of time your application

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.440
can be offline.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.670
This usually depends on SLAs
you offer to your customers.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.180
An SLA is a promise
made by you as a service

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.540
provider to your customers
about the availability

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.880
of your service and
the ramifications

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.760
of failing to deliver the
agreed-upon level of service.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.200
RPO, or recovery
point objective,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.740
is the maximum amount of time
during which the data might

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.410
be lost during an interruption.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.010
Typically, smaller
RTO and RPO values

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.950
mean that the application
must recover quickly

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:43.840
from an interruption.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.710
How quickly a system can recover
after a disaster is defined

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:51.520
by high availability and
disaster recovery patterns,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.710
also known as HA
and DR patterns.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.420
Let's consider this scenario.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.870
I'm making some cakes and
cookies for a party, which

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.850
requires a mixer.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.070
I'm on my first
batch of cookies.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.550
And the mixer starts to
make some weird noises.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:10.479
The manual says that the mixer
will fail with such a noise.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.940
So I need to do
something to carry on

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.836
with the party preparations.

00:02:14.836 --> 00:02:16.540
Hmm.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.050
What do I do now?

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.560
I can call the mixer
company to come

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.490
fix it, which is obviously
going to take time

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:27.670
and won't really work best
given the party is today.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:31.300
Or maybe I can try
to fix it myself

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.600
based on the instructions
in the manual.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.660
Well, this will mean a small
pause in my preparation

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.570
but hmm, will bring
me back on track

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.360
a little bit quicker than
waiting for the mixer repair

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:43.900
guy.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.120
Ha, what else can I do?

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.710
Well, I could also keep
going at a slow-mix option

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:57.160
where I can't really hear that
warning noise, in which case, I

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.450
have to slow down.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.110
But the mixer's still working
so I can continue on and get

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.940
it fixed later.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.310
This option would
definitely have less impact

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.080
on my party preparations,
at least, for today.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.850
So let's review our options
in the DR terminology now.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.430
If we call the mixer
company for a fix,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:19.270
we have to stop making the cake
till they turn up and fix it.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.950
It will be slow
and time consuming.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.500
But eventually, we'll
be back working.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.080
So this scenario is
closest to what we

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.540
could call a cold DR pattern.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.550
If we try and fix
it ourselves, it's

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.410
a bit faster than
the cold pattern.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.230
But since we still need
to pause making our cake

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.710
in order to fix the situation,
it is still slower than normal.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.100
So this scenario is
closest to what we

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.060
would call a warm DR pattern.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.190
And the option where we
continue working at slow speed

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.760
and decide to fix later
is the most efficient,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.450
as I'm still making cakes
without stopping or pausing

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.710
and is closest to what we
would call a hot DR pattern.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.630
Well, moral of
the story, we pick

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.780
a DR pattern that makes sense
for the business at hand.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.360
In this case, it was
really serious business

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.590
of hosting a party.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.440
And look how amazing
this cake turned out.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.780
If you use Google
Cloud for DR, it

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.670
can greatly reduce
the costs that

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:21.320
are associated with achieving
both RTO and RPO values as

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.650
compared to fulfilling those
requirements on-premise.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.170
For example,
traditional DR planning

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.270
requires you to account for
a number of requirements,

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:34.370
including capacity, security,
network infrastructure,

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:36.530
support, and bandwidth.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.510
Google Cloud has
several features

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.990
that help bypass most of
these complicated factors

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.530
and reduces the cost of
managing a DR solution.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.410
Global network, redundancy,
scalability, security,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.920
and compliance are
a few such factors.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.710
Keep following the
series for more on this.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.360
Now let's see what
are some of the best

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.420
practices for planning
a DR strategy that

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.290
can apply to our business.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.330
First and foremost, we want
to define our RTO and RPO

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:08.720
values because they would
indicate an appropriate DR

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.950
pattern.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.880
Then we make sure that we have
a full end-to-end recovery plan.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.640
Just backing up and archiving
data won't be enough.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.740
Make our tasks as
specific as possible

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:23.730
so when the time comes
to execute the plan,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.820
it does not just say,
run the restore script.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.440
Well, from where?

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.710
What's the command?

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.420
Such information should be
there in the instructions.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.280
Implement control measures.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.670
Monitor and send alerts
when something destructive

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:43.160
happens, like spikes in
traffic or deletion in data.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.840
Prepare the software,
verify that you

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.510
can install the
software from the source

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.850
or from a pre-configured image.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.460
Make sure you have the
appropriate licenses

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.930
for that deployment.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.090
Our continuous
deployment toolset

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.370
is an integral part of our
application deployment.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.390
Make sure it's available
at the time of disaster

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.940
to recover the environment.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.760
We should not forget about
security and compliance.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.350
Configure security the same for
DR and production environments.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.610
And last but most
important step,

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.760
make sure your DR plan works.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.620
Maintain multiple paths for data
recovery and test it regularly.

